  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting Shelter Upgrade and Community Building in Bhasan Char and Cox&apos;s Bazar District</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over one million Rohingyas that fled from systematic persecution, discrimination, and other human rights violations in Rakhine State, Myanmar to Cox&apos;s Bazar, Bangladesh currently live in 33 congested camps formally designated by the Government of Bangladesh in Ukhiya and Teknaf Upazilas of Cox&apos;s Bazar District. The host communities also continue to confront issues that contribute to their vulnerabilities related to strained resources, disaster shocks and the environment. Relationships between displaced persons and host communities in Cox&apos;s Bazar vary significantly and are highly dependent on the history and stability of the camps and camp-like settlements. As the situation protracts, however, relations within the different displaced person groupings and between displaced persons and Bangladeshi host communities are expected to increasingly give rise to shifting power dynamics, extortive practices and disputes over access to land, resources and livelihoods. 
 
Building upon the success of past programming for the displaced persons from Myanmar and host communities, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting vulnerable communities through specialized protection services, shelter upgrades, site management and site development in camps and livelihood activities in Bhasan Char. This contributes to the improvement of shelter and community structures in Cox&apos;s Bazar district and Bhasan Char.</narrative>
  </description>
